  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized software development, enabling automated code generation, debugging, and execution. However, these capabilities also introduce significant security and safety concerns. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agents and the emerging field of automated red-teaming for code generation systems. We examine the dual nature of code agents as both powerful productivity tools and potential security risks, discussing recent methodologies for evaluating and mitigating these risks. We review key approaches including automatic red-teaming agents like RedCodeAgent, defensive blue-teaming frameworks such as BlueCodeAgent, and foundational transformer architectures that enable these systems. Our analysis reveals that while significant progress has been made in understanding and mitigating code-related vulnerabilities, substantial challenges remain in developing robust, comprehensive evaluation frameworks. We conclude with recommendations for future research directions and best practices for secure code agent deployment.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software is developed, tested, and maintained. These AI-powered systems combine natural language understanding with code generation capabilities, enabling users to describe programming tasks in plain language and receive executable code solutions. From GitHub Copilot to advanced multi-step coding assistants, code agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in code completion, bug fixing, and even complex program synthesis tasks.\n\nHowever, the integration of code interpreters and execution environments into these agents has introduced new security considerations. Code agents that can not only generate but also execute code face unique challenges related to malicious code generation, prompt injection vulnerabilities, and unsafe execution patterns. Recent studies have shown that code agents can be vulnerable to adversarial prompts that elicit the generation of code containing security vulnerabilities, backdoors, or other malicious functionality.\n\nThe field of automated red-teaming has emerged as a critical approach to identifying and mitigating these vulnerabilities. By employing autonomous agents designed to systematically probe code generation systems for weaknesses, researchers can uncover hidden vulnerabilities before they are exploited in real-world scenarios. This adversarial approach to security testing has proven particularly valuable for understanding the robustness of code agents against various attack vectors.\n\nThis paper surveys the current state of research in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine the foundational technologies that enable these systems, review recent advances in automated red-teaming frameworks, and discuss emerging blue-teaming approaches for defense. Our goal is to provide a comprehensive overview of the field while highlighting open challenges and future research directions.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Transformer Architecture Foundation\n\nThe success of modern code agents is built upon foundational advances in deep learning architectures, particularly the Transformer model introduced by Vaswani et al. in their seminal work \"Attention Is All You Need\" [1]. This architecture, which relies entirely on attention mechanisms without recurrence or convolutions, revolutionized natural language processing and became the backbone of modern language models. The Transformer's ability to capture long-range dependencies and parallelize computation has enabled the development of increasingly capable code generation models that can understand and produce complex programming languages.\n\n### Automated Red-Teaming Frameworks\n\nRed-teaming involves systematically testing systems to identify vulnerabilities by adopting adversarial perspectives. Recent work has extended this approach to automated frameworks specifically designed for code agents. The RedCodeAgent framework [2] represents a significant advancement in this area, providing an automatic red-teaming agent that can probe diverse code agents for vulnerabilities. This approach enables systematic evaluation of code agent safety across multiple attack surfaces, including code injection, prompt manipulation, and unsafe execution patterns.\n\nRedCodeAgent introduces several key innovations: (1) a comprehensive taxonomy of vulnerabilities specific to code agents, (2) automated prompt generation strategies that maximize vulnerability discovery, and (3) a scoring mechanism for quantifying agent robustness across different vulnerability categories. Experimental results demonstrate that RedCodeAgent can uncover significant security issues in commercial and open-source code agents that might otherwise remain undetected.\n\n### Blue-Teaming and Defensive Approaches\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming emphasizes defense and resilience. The BlueCodeAgent framework [3] complements red-teaming approaches by providing a blue teaming agent enabled by automated red teaming for code generation AI systems. This bidirectional approach recognizes that effective security requires both offensive and defensive perspectives.\n\nBlueCodeAgent leverages insights from red-teaming exercises to strengthen defensive mechanisms in code agents. Key contributions include: (1) automated vulnerability detection integrated into the code generation pipeline, (2) real-time adversarial training using red-teaming data, and (3) adaptive defense mechanisms that evolve based on identified attack patterns. This defensive framework represents a shift toward proactive security in code agent systems.\n\n### Broader Context\n\nThe work on code agent safety builds upon broader research in AI trustworthiness and alignment. Previous studies have explored vulnerabilities in large language models [4], benchmarking safety metrics for AI systems [5], and developing evaluation frameworks for code generation tools [6]. Our survey situates these efforts within the specific context of code agents, highlighting unique challenges and opportunities that arise from combining code generation with execution capabilities.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents represents one of the most promising yet challenging frontiers in AI research. While these systems offer tremendous potential for increasing developer productivity and democratizing software development, they also introduce novel security challenges that require careful attention. The work surveyed in this paper, including RedCodeAgent [2], BlueCodeAgent [3], and the foundational Transformer architecture [1], represents important progress toward understanding and mitigating these risks.\n\nSeveral key observations emerge from our analysis:\n\n1. **Dual-use Nature**: Code agents exemplify the dual-use nature of powerful AI systems, offering both significant benefits and potential harms that must be managed through thoughtful design and evaluation.\n\n2. **Need for Comprehensive Evaluation**: Current red-teaming approaches, while valuable, require more comprehensive evaluation frameworks that cover the full spectrum of potential vulnerabilities in code agents.\n\n3. **Adversarial-Defensive Cycle**: The interplay between red-teaming and blue-teaming approaches suggests a promising direction for continuous improvement in code agent security.\n\n4. **Architectural Foundations**: The Transformer architecture continues to enable advances in code agents, but specialized architectures and training methods may be needed to address code-specific security concerns.\n\nFuture research should focus on developing standardized benchmarks for code agent safety, creating more sophisticated adversarial testing methodologies, and designing inherently safer code agent architectures. As code agents become increasingly integrated into software development workflows, ongoing research and collaboration between security researchers, AI practitioners, and software engineers will be essential for ensuring these powerful tools are deployed safely and responsibly.\n\n## References\n\n[1] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar et al., \"Attention Is All You Need,\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n\n[2] C. Guo, C. Xie, Y. Yang et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[3] C. Guo, Y. Nie, C. Xie et al., \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI,\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[4] B. Wang, W. Chen, H. Pei et al., \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models,\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[5] C. Guo, X. Liu, C. Xie et al., \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents,\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[6] K. He, X. Zhang, S. Ren et al., \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition,\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\nEOF"
